solutions.explanation:
Since \(x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1)\), the original equation is defined only for \(x\ne1,-1\). Subtracting the right side from the left lets us test whether they can be equal. For every allowed \(x\),
\[
\frac{1}{x-1}-\frac{2}{x^2-1}
=\frac{x+1-2}{(x-1)(x+1)}
=\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x+1)}
=\frac{1}{x+1}.
\]
The cancellation is valid because \(x\ne1\). The final fraction has numerator \(1\) and a nonzero denominator, so it cannot equal zero. Thus the two sides are never equal, and the original equation has no solution, choice C.
